package pulseengine:falcon-cascade@0.12.0;

/// Falcon control cascade — Component Model edition.
///
/// Each controller is a typed component exporting one interface.
/// The `cascade` world imports all five and exports the top-level
/// `step`; `wac` wires the graph (falcon-cascade.wac). This is the
/// v0.7 rework: the v0.6 raw-`#[export_name]` scalar shortcut is
/// replaced by real Component Model components so the cascade
/// composes declaratively.
///
/// Records use flat f32 fields (not tuple<>) — the canonical ABI is
/// then a flat scalar list, which keeps both wac composition and the
/// eventual synth → ARM lowering simple.

interface types {
    /// EKF state estimate. quaternion is body-to-NED (w,x,y,z);
    /// position/velocity are NED metres / m·s⁻¹; body-rates wx/wy/wz
    /// are the gyro reading (rad/s) passed through for the rate loop.
    record vehicle-state {
        qw: f32, qx: f32, qy: f32, qz: f32,
        pos-n: f32, pos-e: f32, pos-d: f32,
        vel-n: f32, vel-e: f32, vel-d: f32,
        wx: f32, wy: f32, wz: f32,
        innovation: f32,
    }

    /// FLIGHT STATE PUBLISHED FOR OBSERVERS — the RETURN direction of the
    /// seam (SWREQ-FALCON-TRANSPORT-P01). `step` hands back motor commands and
    /// nothing else, so the verified MAVLink telemetry stack had nothing to
    /// read, no log could be written, and a shadow flight produced no
    /// comparable evidence. This is what an observer reads.
    ///
    /// WHY u32 AND NOT u64 (integrator request, jess#167, taken before any
    /// publish). `LDRD` is NOT single-copy atomic on ARMv7-M, so a 64-bit
    /// counter can tear mid-read and the two halves can land such that head
    /// coincidentally equals tail — the seqlock then reports coherent on a torn
    /// body. A 32-bit load is single-copy atomic, so the comparison means what
    /// it says. An in-process harness cannot observe this, which is exactly why
    /// the integrator's architecture knowledge outranks our measurement here.
    /// Wrap is handled by comparing equality, never ordering.
    ///
    /// SEQLOCK. `tick-head` is written FIRST and `tick-tail` LAST, so a reader
    /// on another core reads tail, then the body, then head, and RETRIES if the
    /// two counters disagree. Shape specified by the integrator (jess#167):
    /// without it a second export can return a body that never corresponded to
    /// any single tick — a torn read across the core boundary, which on a
    /// cached OCRAM region yields stale numbers with NO fault at all.
    ///
    /// FIXED LAYOUT, NO `option<>`, NO LISTS. Absence is carried by `valid`,
    /// a bitmask, not by a discriminant whose position moves. Variable-shape
    /// data across a cache boundary is exactly where tearing bites.
    ///
    /// The tick is MONOTONIC and never resets, so a reader can judge staleness
    /// against its own clock instead of trusting freshness — and the CADENCE is
    /// deliberately unspecified here. jess declined to invent one ("no falcon
    /// code has executed on an RT1176"), so the observed rate is computed from
    /// the tick delta on the first shadow flight rather than agreed by two
    /// parties who have not measured it.
    record flight-state {
        /// Seqlock head. Equal to `tick-tail` iff the body is coherent.
        tick-head: u32,
        /// Body→NED attitude (unit quaternion, Hamilton scalar-first).
        qw: f32, qx: f32, qy: f32, qz: f32,
        /// NED position (m); `pos-d` is negative up.
        pos-n: f32, pos-e: f32, pos-d: f32,
        /// NED velocity (m/s).
        vel-n: f32, vel-e: f32, vel-d: f32,
        /// Body rates (rad/s).
        wx: f32, wy: f32, wz: f32,
        /// Estimated gyro bias (rad/s) — a shadow flight compares this against
        /// the reference filter's, and it is where a divergence shows first.
        bgx: f32, bgy: f32, bgz: f32,
        /// Estimator innovation magnitude — the trust signal.
        innovation: f32,
        /// Isolated failed rotor, 0-3; 0xFF when none. Flat, not `option<u8>`.
        failed-rotor: u8,
        /// FLIGHT MODE (SUPERVISOR-P01). The `relay-fsm` discriminant:
        ///   0 disarmed  1 armed  2 takeoff  3 loiter
        ///   4 mission   5 land   6 rtl      7 terminated
        ///
        /// `disarmed` AND `terminated` ARE DISTINCT AND MUST STAY SO.
        /// `disarmed` is a SAFE on-ground state; `terminated` is a deliberate
        /// AIRBORNE thrust cut that nothing recovers from without an on-ground
        /// reset. A decoder that collapses them reports a terminated vehicle as
        /// safely disarmed — the absence-looks-like-data shape this project has
        /// already paid for twice (a battery read as healthy, an accelerometer
        /// read as gravity).
        ///
        /// Placed HERE deliberately: this byte lands in padding that already
        /// existed between `failed-rotor` and the u32s, so MODE COSTS ZERO
        /// BYTES. The record grows 84 -> 88 B, all of it `failsafe`.
        mode: u8,
        /// ACTIVE FAILSAFE CAUSES (SUPERVISOR-P01) — a bitmask, not an enum,
        /// because several are live at once and the supervisor's own breach
        /// term ORs geofence with battery, so an enum would have to pick a
        /// winner and discard the rest.
        ///   bit 0 rtl-latched     1 batt-low        2 batt-critical
        ///   bit 3 batt-silent     4 geofence-breach 5 runaway-arming
        ///   bit 6 wind-arming     7 arm-blocked
        ///
        /// `mode` is the OUTCOME; this is the CAUSE. Without it a consumer sees
        /// the vehicle divert and cannot tell a flat pack from a geofence
        /// breach from a wind failsafe. Mirrors
        /// `FlightSupervisor::failsafe_flags` exactly; every bit is a condition
        /// `step` already acts on.
        failsafe: u32,
        /// Which fields above are meaningful this tick. Bit 0 attitude,
        /// 1 position, 2 velocity, 3 rates, 4 gyro bias, 5 innovation,
        /// 6 failed-rotor, 7 mode, 8 failsafe. A clear bit means NOT MEASURED,
        /// never "zero".
        valid: u32,
        /// Seqlock tail, written last. See `tick-head`.
        tick-tail: u32,
    }

    /// Raw IMU sample — body-frame accel (m/s²) + gyro (rad/s).
    record imu-sample {
        ax: f32, ay: f32, az: f32,
        gx: f32, gy: f32, gz: f32,
    }

    /// Attitude setpoint — body-to-NED quaternion + collective thrust.
    record attitude-setpoint {
        qw: f32, qx: f32, qy: f32, qz: f32,
        thrust: f32,
    }

    /// Body-rate setpoint (rad/s) + collective thrust.
    record rate-setpoint {
        rx: f32, ry: f32, rz: f32,
        thrust: f32,
    }

    /// Body-frame torque setpoint (N·m) + collective thrust.
    record torque-setpoint {
        tx: f32, ty: f32, tz: f32,
        thrust: f32,
    }

    /// Per-motor PWM for the falcon-quad airframe, each in [0, 1].
    record motor-pwm {
        m1: f32, m2: f32, m3: f32, m4: f32,
    }

    /// Mission waypoint — NED target position + heading.
    record waypoint {
        north: f32, east: f32, down: f32, yaw: f32,
    }

    /// A 3-vector; the frame is named by the field that uses it.
    record vec3 {
        x: f32, y: f32, z: f32,
    }

    /// Everything the flight core needs for ONE tick.
    ///
    /// v0.8 replaces the bare `imu-sample`, because v0.7 could not express two
    /// things a host must be able to say — both found by actually driving the
    /// published components rather than by reading the interface.
    ///
    /// `dt-s` — the host's ACTUAL control period. v0.7 had no way to state it,
    /// so every stage hardcoded its own and they disagreed with each other by up
    /// to 20x (estimator 1 ms, position 20 ms, attitude 4 ms, rate 1 ms) while
    /// the cascade called all five once per tick. Measured on the SITL plant,
    /// holding 2.0 m: 1000 Hz -> 0.00 m error, 400 Hz -> 10.43 m, 250 Hz -> 27.17 m,
    /// with no error signal of any kind.
    ///
    /// The aiding measurements — v0.7 took the IMU alone, so position was
    /// observable only by dead reckoning. The flight core fuses GNSS, the
    /// magnetometer and an absolute heading; a seam that cannot carry them
    /// cannot carry the flight core. Every field is `option<>`, and all-`none`
    /// is a valid IMU-only host that simply inherits the drift.
    record sensor-frame {
        imu: imu-sample,
        dt-s: f32,
        /// NED position fix (m) — GNSS or equivalent.
        position-ned: option<vec3>,
        /// Body-frame magnetometer. Any consistent unit; normalised internally.
        mag-body: option<vec3>,
        /// Absolute heading (rad, NED). An alternative to `mag-body` for hosts
        /// that already resolve yaw; both correct the same unobservable state.
        heading-rad: option<f32>,
        /// Per-rotor ESC telemetry (bidirectional DShot, or equivalent).
        ///
        /// v0.10. Without it the rotor-out FDI is INERT: a rotor loss goes
        /// undetected, because the information never crosses the boundary. The
        /// native backend has always fed it; the seam simply had no field, so
        /// the capability was verified in falcon-core and unreachable from a
        /// component — the same orphan-signal shape as the hardcoded tick
        /// period and the missing calibration.
        ///
        /// Note this was invisible to the wasm/native differential too: both
        /// sides received `none` and agreed perfectly. An equivalence test is
        /// blind to a channel neither side receives.
        ///
        /// `none` = no telemetry, and the FDI falls back to its RPM-free path.
        motor-rpm: option<rotor-rpm>,
        /// Pack voltage (V), or `none` when the host has no battery sense.
        ///
        /// AN OPTION, NOT A SENTINEL, and that is the load-bearing part. The
        /// supervisor declares the pack ON FIRST SIGHT: until a reading arrives
        /// `batt_seen` is false and a silent pack CANNOT raise a failsafe,
        /// which is what stops "no sense at all" reading as "the pack died". A
        /// 0.0 sentinel would destroy that distinction, and a healthy default
        /// would restore the fail-unsafe behaviour already corrected once.
        ///
        /// WITHOUT THIS FIELD the supervisor's battery failsafe is STRUCTURALLY
        /// DEAD: `FlightBackend::read_battery_v` defaults to `None`, so
        /// `batt_seen` never becomes true and BATTERY-P01 / BATTERY-P03 — both
        /// verified in the core — are unreachable through the seam.
        battery-v: option<f32>,
    }

    /// Per-rotor measured speed, in the airframe's motor order — the same order
    /// as `motor-pwm`. A flat record rather than a list: no allocation, and it
    /// lowers to a flat scalar sequence like every other record here.
    record rotor-rpm {
        m1: s32, m2: s32, m3: s32, m4: s32,
    }

    /// Airframe calibration — the numbers the control law needs and CANNOT
    /// derive from the sensor stream.
    ///
    /// v0.9 exists because the v0.8 component could fly NOTHING but its own
    /// defaults: every value below was frozen at construction, so no host could
    /// correct them from outside and no amount of retuning could make the
    /// published component hold altitude on a real plant.
    ///
    /// MEASURED on the gz falcon-quad, 2 m hold, 2000 ticks @ 250 Hz, one
    /// knob at a time against the tuned native reference (0.16 m hold error):
    ///
    ///   hover-thrust 0.585 -> 0.5, tuning kept  : 2.65 m, est_z -58.8 m
    ///   tuning dropped, hover-thrust kept       : 0.66 m, est_z  -1.3 m
    ///   both (i.e. exactly the v0.8 defaults)   : 1.91 m, never left the ground
    ///
    /// Both groups are load-bearing and `hover-thrust` dominates: at 0.5 the
    /// airframe cannot lift, the estimator is then driven by a specific force
    /// that never matches the commanded one, and the altitude estimate diverges
    /// by an order of magnitude. That is the failure mode a host MUST be able
    /// to correct, and before v0.9 it could not.
    record vehicle-config {
        /// Normalised collective throttle that holds THIS airframe in a steady
        /// hover. The dominant term — see the measurements above. gz
        /// falcon-quad 0.585; the SITL mock plant 0.49.
        hover-thrust: f32,
        /// Control-loop rate (Hz). AUTHORITATIVE: the estimator and the ADRC
        /// observer are discretised on it. `sensor-frame.dt-s` remains the
        /// per-tick integration step (it varies); this is the design rate. A
        /// host that supplies neither inherits a guess, which is how the v0.7
        /// stages ended up 20x apart.
        loop-rate-hz: f32,
        /// Position-measurement variance (m²). The default over-trusts a real
        /// GNSS: on a long static hover the covariance collapses, the outlier
        /// gate then rejects correct fixes, and the estimate goes deaf. 0.25
        /// for the gz NavSat.
        pos-var: f32,
        /// Additive process-noise floor (variance/s) on velocity. Keeps the
        /// δv↔δp correlation alive so a long hover cannot starve the filter.
        process-floor-vel: f32,
        /// Additive process-noise floor (variance/s) on position.
        process-floor-pos: f32,
        /// Altitude-loop proportional gain.
        altitude-kp: f32,
        /// Altitude-loop derivative gain.
        altitude-kd: f32,
        /// Altitude-loop integral gain, trimming the residual hover-feedforward
        /// error. Safe only once the loop is damped and the estimate tracks.
        altitude-ki: f32,
        /// Horizontal position-loop integral gain. 0 disables it.
        position-ki: f32,

        // ── SUPERVISOR CONFIGURATION (SUPERVISOR-P01) ──────────────────────
        //
        // `FlightSupervisor::new(home, fence-radius, cruise-alt, low-batt-v)`.
        // The seam carried NONE of these, so the published component could only
        // ever run a bare FlightCore. A supervisor on defaults is worse than no
        // supervisor, which is why these land in the SAME change that wires it
        // in rather than in a follow-up.
        /// Home / launch point, NED metres: the geofence centre and the RTL
        /// destination.
        home-n: f32,
        home-e: f32,
        home-d: f32,
        /// Geofence radius in metres about `home`. Exceeding it latches an RTL.
        fence-radius: f32,
        /// RTL cruise altitude, NED metres (negative is up).
        cruise-alt: f32,
        /// Pack LOW threshold in volts for the WHOLE battery, not per cell; the
        /// estimator derives its per-cell fallbacks from this.
        low-batt-v: f32,
    }
}

/// State estimator — wraps relay-ekf (Mahony complementary filter).
interface ekf {
    use types.{imu-sample, vehicle-state};
    estimate: func(imu: imu-sample) -> vehicle-state;
}

/// Position controller — wraps relay-pos.
interface position {
    use types.{vehicle-state, waypoint, attitude-setpoint};
    tick: func(state: vehicle-state, target: waypoint) -> attitude-setpoint;
}

/// Attitude controller — wraps relay-att.
interface attitude {
    use types.{vehicle-state, attitude-setpoint, rate-setpoint};
    tick: func(state: vehicle-state, sp: attitude-setpoint) -> rate-setpoint;
}

/// Rate controller — wraps relay-rate (body-rate PID).
interface rate {
    use types.{vehicle-state, rate-setpoint, torque-setpoint};
    tick: func(state: vehicle-state, sp: rate-setpoint) -> torque-setpoint;
}

/// Control allocator — wraps relay-mix-quad (X-config mixer).
interface mixer {
    use types.{torque-setpoint, motor-pwm};
    mix: func(torque: torque-setpoint) -> motor-pwm;
}

// ── leaf component worlds — one controller each ───────────────────
world ekf-component      { export ekf; }
world position-component { export position; }
world attitude-component { export attitude; }
world rate-component     { export rate; }
world mixer-component    { export mixer; }

// ── cascade orchestrator ──────────────────────────────────────────
/// Top-level cascade entry point. A single-func interface (rather
/// than a bare world-level export) so the cascade component is
/// structurally uniform with the five leaf components — and so the
/// wit-bindgen export macro is generated the same, interface-shaped
/// way the leaf components rely on.
interface controller {
    use types.{sensor-frame, waypoint, motor-pwm, vehicle-config};
    /// One full flight-core tick: estimate, fuse whatever aiding the frame
    /// carries, then geometric SE(3) attitude → ADRC rate → mixer, with the
    /// rotor-out FDI and the altitude/position loops the vehicle actually flies.
    step: func(sensors: sensor-frame, target: waypoint) -> motor-pwm;

    /// Install this vehicle's calibration.
    ///
    /// Call ONCE before the first `step`. Calling it later is legal and
    /// RE-INITIALISES the control core — estimator state included — because
    /// `loop-rate-hz` is fixed at construction and a partially-applied
    /// configuration is worse than an explicit reset. A host that never calls
    /// this keeps the built-in defaults, which fly the SITL mock plant and
    /// nothing else.
    configure: func(cfg: vehicle-config);
}

/// Imports the five controller interfaces, exports `controller`.
/// The implementation calls estimate → position → attitude → rate
/// → mixer in order. wac satisfies the imports from the leaf
/// components (BUILD.bazel `wac_plug`).
/// v0.8: the cascade component wraps falcon-core's FlightCore directly, so it
/// imports NOTHING. The five-stage decomposition it used to compose was a
/// wasm-side invention that never corresponded to the flight architecture —
/// which is exactly how it drifted onto the legacy PID controllers while
/// falcon-core moved to geometric SE(3) + ADRC, unnoticed for three months
/// (#388). One component, one tick, one clock, and the same control law the
/// vehicle flies.
/// THE RETURN DIRECTION (SWREQ-FALCON-TRANSPORT-P01).
///
/// A SECOND EXPORT, deliberately not a return value on `step`. jess#167: on the
/// M4 you must be able to read state WITHOUT running a control tick, or the act
/// of observing executes the controller and changes what it observes. It also
/// keeps `step`'s lowered symbol byte-stable, which matters because jess's
/// baselines are byte-identity claims about lowered objects.
interface observer {
    use types.{flight-state};

    /// The most recent published state. Cheap, side-effect-free, and safe to
    /// call at a cadence unrelated to the control loop.
    ///
    /// Read it as a seqlock: compare `tick-tail` against `tick-head` and retry
    /// if they differ. A single in-process caller cannot observe a tear, but a
    /// reader on another core across shared memory can, and that is the case
    /// this shape exists for.
    read-state: func() -> flight-state;
}

world cascade {
    export controller;
    export observer;
}
